# sat_add.sby — the D9 saturating-counter law (spec/counters.md §4).
#
# Five sites implement it and none of them can be tested: the counters are u64
# and the rail is ~2^64 events away, so the saturating arm is unreachable in
# simulation. Measured, not supposed -- breaking the headroom test outright
# passes cmd_dma_directed AND its 5,000-packet random lane. That is why the
# five hand-written copies became one `zhao_pkg::zhao_sat_add{64,32}` and why
# the evidence for it is here rather than in tests/differential/.
#
# TWO TASKS, both required (ledger rule V16):
#   bmc    — the result is the exact sum clamped, never wraps, is identity at
#            b = 0, and the rail is absorbing.
#   cover  — the saturating arm is REACHABLE, along with the exact boundary
#            (a + b == MAX) and one past it. Without this the properties are
#            satisfied by an adder that never saturates, which is the shape
#            MEM.GUARD's lane once failed in.
#
# MUTATION SWEEP: 7 attempted, 7 accounted, 6 caught, 1 EQUIVALENT.
#
# The one that matters is M1, `~b` replaced by plain `b`, which removes the
# headroom test altogether: the bmc task fails it, and cmd_dma_directed plus a
# 5,000-packet random lane both pass it. That gap is the reason this file
# exists. Also caught: the rail off by one (both widths), saturation deleted
# outright, the headroom test taken on the wrong operand, and `a` compared
# against itself.
#
# M3, `>` widened to `>=`, SURVIVES AND IS EQUIVALENT -- recorded here rather
# than left looking like a hole. The two spellings differ only at a == ~b,
# which is exactly a + b == MAX; there the original computes a + b == MAX and
# the mutant returns MAX, the same value. So no input distinguishes them and
# no test can. That case is not merely untested, either: c_exact_boundary
# covers it, so the solver produces a witness reaching it.
## SCOPE-TOTAL: the harness is PURELY COMBINATIONAL and every input is free, so
# depth 1 evaluates the properties over the ENTIRE input space -- all 2^128
# pairs for the 64-bit law and 2^64 for the 32-bit one, symbolically. There is
# no state, no horizon and nothing a deeper run could reach that depth 1 does
# not, so the a_scope_*/a_horizon_* guard rule V19 asks for has nothing to pin;
# this waiver is the alternative V19 allows for exactly that case.
#
# Frontend: read_slang reading the REAL frozen zhao_pkg.sv -- the proof is
# about the shipped function, not a copy of it beside the test.
#
# Run via `ctest -L formal` (test formal_sat_add) or by hand with
# cwd = tests/formal:
#   sby -f --prefix <workdir> sat_add.sby

[tasks]
bmc
cover

[options]
bmc:   mode bmc
bmc:   depth 1
cover: mode cover
cover: depth 1
expect pass

[engines]
btor btormc

[script]
read_slang --top zhao_sat_add_harness -DFORMAL zhao_abi_pkg.sv zhao_pkg.sv sat_add_harness.sv
prep -top zhao_sat_add_harness

[files]
../../fpga/rtl/generated/zhao_abi_pkg.sv
../../fpga/rtl/common/zhao_pkg.sv
sat_add_harness.sv
